.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_count (void)"

.PP
Counts the cameras this machine has\&. Zero is an ordinary answer, not a failure\&. A desktop with no webcam, a machine whose camera is switched off in firmware, and a phone with the camera disabled by policy all report none\&.
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many cameras there are\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_camera_device (uint32_t index)"

.PP
Returns the identifier of the camera at a position in the list\&. The identifier is what everything else here takes\&. Positions are not stable across a device being plugged in or unplugged; identifiers are, for as long as the device is present\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIindex\fP From zero to schultz_camera_count minus one\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The identifier, or zero when there is no camera there\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_name (uint64_t device, char * out_name, uint64_t size)"

.PP
Copies a camera's name, the one a person would recognise\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdevice\fP An identifier from schultz_camera_device\&. 
.br
\fIout_name\fP Receives the name, NUL terminated and truncated to fit\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIsize\fP How many bytes out_name holds\&. Must be at least one\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when there is no such camera\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_facing (uint64_t device)"

.PP
Reports which way a camera points\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdevice\fP An identifier from schultz_camera_device\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of the SCHULTZ_CAMERA_FACING_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_size_count (uint64_t device)"

.PP
How many sizes a camera offers\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdevice\fP An identifier from schultz_camera_device\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The count, or zero when the camera will not say\&. A camera is allowed to answer nothing: on some platforms it tells nobody anything until it has been opened once\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_size_at (uint64_t device, uint32_t index, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height, uint32_t * out_rate)"

.PP
Reads one of the sizes a camera offers\&. \fBAsk before opening\&.\fP A camera converts anything it is given, so opening at a size it does not have works and is the wrong thing to do twice over: the conversion is done in software on every frame, and a phone sensor's own size is enormous\&. A twelve megapixel frame is forty eight megabytes of pixels, which is measured in seconds per picture rather than pictures per second\&. Every phone offers something small natively; this is how to find it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdevice\fP An identifier from schultz_camera_device\&. 
.br
\fIindex\fP Which one, from zero to schultz_camera_size_count - 1\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_rate\fP Receives pictures per second, rounded, or zero where the camera did not say\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when there is no such camera or no such index\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_open (uint64_t device, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t rate, \fBschultz_camera\fP ** out_camera)"

.PP
Opens a camera\&. The size is what the pictures are wanted at\&. \fBChoose one the camera offers\fP, using schultz_camera_size_count and schultz_camera_size_at\&. A camera that cannot produce the size asked for converts every frame in software instead, which costs more than the drawing does and has been seen to produce a torn picture\&. Passing zero for both takes the camera's own size, which on a phone is the whole sensor and far larger than anything worth previewing\&.
.PP
Opening does not mean the camera is usable yet\&. See schultz_camera_permission\&.
.PP
\fBName a rate, or let this pick the fastest\&.\fP Passing zero here does not mean 'no preference' to the layer underneath: it reads an unset rate as zero and then chooses the rate closest to it, which is the \fIslowest\fP the camera offers\&. A phone lists the bottom of every range as well as the top, so that is often one picture a second, and the session is then pinned to it\&. This call therefore never leaves the rate unset: zero here means the fastest offered at the size chosen, and any other number is matched to the nearest the camera has\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdevice\fP An identifier from schultz_camera_device\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP The width wanted, or zero for the camera's own\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP The height wanted, or zero for the camera's own\&. 
.br
\fIrate\fP Pictures per second wanted, or zero for the fastest the camera offers at that size\&. 
.br
\fIout_camera\fP Receives the open camera\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when there is no such camera or the machine will not open it\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_rate (const \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
How many pictures a second the camera settled on\&. What was actually negotiated, which is not always what was asked for\&. Zero until the camera has been allowed and has chosen, so a program that wants to show it asks again rather than once\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Pictures per second, rounded, or zero when it is not settled yet\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_camera_close (\fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
Closes a camera and releases it\&. Safe on NULL\&. The pixels from the last schultz_camera_frame belong to the camera and are gone after this\&.
.PP
\fBOn the thread that runs the tree\fP, this is safe to call while a preview is still showing the camera, and in any order with destroying that preview\&. A camera knows which previews are pointed at it and tells them when it goes, so a stop button needs no particular sequence\&. The preview keeps whatever it last drew on the screen and simply stops asking for more; point it somewhere else, or hide it, to clear that\&.
.PP
\fBFrom any other thread it is not safe, and the failure is quiet\&.\fP A preview reads its camera on every turn, and there is no lock between the two: closing from a worker while the tree is being advanced can free the camera between the turn's check and its use\&. What happens then is the platform's business -- on a desktop it faults, and on a phone it can block instead, inside a lock belonging to memory that has been handed back, which shows as an interface that never draws again rather than as a crash\&.
.PP
Close it from the thread that runs the window, like everything else that touches the tree\&. If the decision to stop is made elsewhere, carry it over as a message and act on it there\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP The camera to close\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_permission (const \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
Asks whether the person has allowed this camera to be used\&. Ask each turn of the loop until it stops saying waiting\&. There is nothing to do in the meantime and nothing a host can do to hurry it: the answer is the operating system's and the person's\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_CAMERA_REFUSED\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of the SCHULTZ_CAMERA_* permission values\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_size (const \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height)"

.PP
Returns the size the pictures actually come at\&. Known once permission has been given, and not always the size that was asked for\&. Both are zero before then\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_frame (\fBschultz_camera\fP * camera, const uint32_t ** out_pixels, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height, uint64_t * out_when_ns)"

.PP
Takes the newest picture, if one has arrived\&. Premultiplied ARGB, one word a pixel, width by height with no padding between rows\&. Whatever the camera's own format was, and whatever had to be done to it, is already done\&.
.PP
The pixels belong to the camera and stay valid until the next call to this for the same camera, or until it is closed\&. A host that needs to keep one copies it\&.
.PP
Returning SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED means no new picture since the last call, which is the ordinary answer most times it is asked: a camera at thirty a second has nothing new on most turns of a loop running at sixty\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_pixels\fP Receives the pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_when_ns\fP Receives when the picture was taken, in nanoseconds on the platform's own clock\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there is nothing new, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_camera_frames_taken (const \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
Counts the pictures taken since the camera was opened\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many pictures have been handed over\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_camera_frames_skipped (const \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
Counts the pictures passed over to get to a newer one\&. A camera goes on producing whether or not anything is looking, and what matters to a preview is the newest picture rather than every picture\&. So asking for one takes whatever has piled up and answers with the last of them; the ones in front are counted here\&.
.PP
Which makes this the answer to 'is this program keeping up?'\&. Zero, or rising slowly, means it is\&. Rising as fast as the camera's own rate means every second picture is being passed over, and so on\&. It is the number to look at when a preview feels late rather than slow\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcamera\fP An open camera\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many pictures have been passed over\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_preview_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a node that shows what a camera sees\&. The node takes the size the layout gives it and draws the picture to fit, keeping the picture's own proportions, exactly as a video node does\&. It is an ordinary node: laid out, clipped and hit tested like any other\&.
.PP
It does the waiting for permission on the host's behalf\&. Before the answer comes it draws nothing; if the answer is no it goes on drawing nothing, and schultz_camera_permission on the camera says which\&.
.PP
A node with no camera asks for no clock at all, so a preview that is not showing anything costs nothing\&.
.PP
The camera is not owned by the node\&. It must outlive it, and closing it while a node still points at it leaves the node showing its last picture\&.
.PP
\fBThe preview asks to be the shape of the pictures\&.\fP A camera's shape is not something a host can know in advance: a phone turns its pictures upright, so a stream that is sixteen by nine on the wire arrives nine by sixteen, and which it is depends on how the phone is being held\&. So when the shape changes the preview sets its own preferred height, from whatever width the host asked for and the picture's proportions\&. The width is left alone, because how wide to be is the host's decision\&.
.PP
A host that also fixes the height, or caps it with a maximum size, still wins: this is a preference and a maximum outranks it\&. That is also the reason a preview still letterboxed after all this is a host holding its height down\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIcamera\fP The camera to show, or NULL for none yet\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_camera_preview_set_camera (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_camera\fP * camera)"

.PP
Points a preview node at a different camera, or at none\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_camera_preview_create\&. 
.br
\fIcamera\fP The camera to show, or NULL to show nothing\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

